t into trouble exactly when we  borrowed a lot and then the environment  changes and a lot of the borrowing that  looked like it was going to be repaid  easily isn't and that's essentially 2008  it's the the household borrowed up to  the hilt because they thought well  housing prices would keep on going up so  it makes sense to borrow the bank's  loaned and they also borrowed financial  institutions borrowed and they were  partly able to evade regulations because  the regulations were outdated with  back to you know how much you had to  hold in reserve as capital so you had a  combination of financial deregulation  that's the sort of direct government  interventions you had the government  itself embarking on a spending spree  after the 2001 tax cuts we cut taxes get  in 2003 and that encouraged more  borrowing and we had financial  innovation and all these three things  led to increase in borrowing by all  sectors of the economy and it all worked  fantastically to give us this period of  prosperity until of course it didn't  work that is people said oh let's  reassess whether housing prices will  keep on going up oh those securities  that we issued well I don't think  they're worth quite as much as we  thought or the regulators thought and  the whole system starts unraveling in  Reverse the political response to 2008  Ben Bernanke Henry Paulson Sheila Bair  Presidents Bush and Obama did they get  it right I think once the crisis  unfolded definitely Ben Bernanke and the  Federal Reserve acted correctly and  that's no smart small measure in do to  the part that Bernanke's doctoral theses  had to do with the Great Depression and  the fact that you know he worked out a  new interpretation of why the Great  Depression was so severe and that was  because the collapse of banking sector  and this view was slightly actually a  big departure from the dominant view  that was associated with Milton Friedman  which had talked about the monetary  influences Bernanke talked about the  credit influences said you really have  to rescue the financial system to make  sure that lending continues and so I  gave him a lot of credit for saving  things the Bush administration did push  through a small stimulus package in 2008  and that probably helped a bit but  really in terms of fiscal policy tarp  which was the bank bailout was  incredibly important because it  reassured people in the financial sector  along with the Federal Reserve actions  shirred people that the banking system  was going to continue okay essentially  tarp was a recapitalization of the  banking system the banks had been  holding lots of assets that suddenly  were less valuable than they originally  thought if that happened sufficiently  then the banks are insolvent and they  either shut down or the continuous like  zombie banks as they did in Japan for a  while and so you need somebody to come  in to provide funds to make those banks  solvent and that's what the US  government did if it hadn't then I would  think that you know the financial system  would have ground to a halt eventually  it would not immediately but it would  have stopped lending and you can't run  in finance you can't run an economy  actually without a financial system  that's working  professor Qin our recessions and  economic downturns natural occurrences I  think they're natural occurrences  because you are always going to have a  confluence of events that that strike  economies that we're going to push them  down or up and so cycles are going to  occur you don't want to think them as  sort of mechanical so you at the moment  you hear about people saying this  recovery is unnaturally long it's longer  than the average and so it must by  definition come to an end my view would  be at some time you know either the  Federal Reserve acts in a way that tips  the economy into recession or some shock  strikes that forces the feds hands or  some shock combination of shocks from  overseas just induces the economy t