o borrow up in large  amounts only putting in a little little  bit of their own shareholders money and  borrow up using a sort of short-term  financial market instruments which those  are the two sort of things that got the  financial system into trouble before and  I think with implementation of  dodd-frank and sort of international  regulations on banking so what are  called on the rubric of Basel three that  you've actually made the banking system  a lot more stable there still remains  some holes in the regulation of the  international  system and I'd say that it's now one of  the things is we fix problems in the  sectors where we've seen the problems  it's likely that the next problem we see  is someplace that we didn't expect it or  we didn't apply regulations I mean one  way of thinking about 2008 is you know  it wasn't the the mainline banks that  were the big problem we regulated them  in 1930s in response to the problems of  the Great Depression  but then we encountered a whole set of  problems in the shadow financial system  because individuals and firms and banks  and other financial institutions had  worked their way around those  regulations or you know here's the term  of financial innovation they had  innovated the way around the regulations  and so we put in the whole set of  regulations now I think it's made the  system safer for a period of time more  robust more stable but people are always  going to be looking around for a way of  evading the regulations so that they can  get a higher rate of return and at the  same time impose the risks upon the  greater financial system and that's that  the hazard that regulators keep on  having to try to keep up with those who  are trying to evade the regulations is  that the message you want people who  read lost the lost decades to take away  the lessons that's exactly one of them  it's that you can't just say we've had  this one big crisis we fix things now  move on history is replete with these  examples where individuals households  firms banks whatever all want to  leverage up because that's a way of  making higher return than you would  otherwise and they want to leverage up  borrow a lot and there's a tendency  because it induces a at least in the  short term a period of buoyant growth  and returns  there's always incentive to say well  there's no trouble there's no problem at  the point at this point if I'm a  politician it's going to land into  somebody else's lap in the future if I'm  a household you might say I'll get out  just before everything crashes or  there's a tendency to say times are  different now  we're just smarter or more nimble than  we used to be the old rules don't apply  and I think our main point is the old  rules to large extent do still apply if  you try to borrow a lot leverage up a  lot then inevitably some correction will  come round and a lot of pain is going to  be inflicted upon a lot of and many  times innocent bystanders what are you  teaching here at the University of  Wisconsin and what do you want your  students after a semester with you to  leave with so I teach mostly  macroeconomics so international  macroeconomics to both undergraduates in  the Economics Department and I teach  sort of an international macro course  two masters level students in the  LaFollette School of Public Affairs and  the the one thing that I want them to  know is keep alert to read about what's  going on in the world and incorporate  what you've learned in class to  analyzing why is it that you know things  are the way they are why is it that  certain individuals in certain positions  of management in financial firms say  what they say versus what people in  policy positions would say versus what  journalists would say in particular to  use their minds to analyze and dissect  what's being stated and argued rather  than taking for granted you know  somebody says something and because they  look authoritative believe it I want  them to question question everything and  to use their minds to analyze what's  going around going on around them all  right macroeconomics why can a small  country like Greece mm-hmm affect our  giant economy here in the US  so that's interesting question and it is  let me give you an analogue there was a  interesting statement about that the  construction market or construction in  the u.s. leading up to 2008 people would  say well construction of housing only  counts for a small portion of GDP how  can it affect the rest of the economy  and I think what we've learned over the  past eight nine years is  interconnections matter and in in  particular in the case of housing it  wasn't just the actual act of building  housing but it was the fact that people  owned assets that were involved in  housing or houses and that assets were  derived or derivatives had their value  on the basis of housing prices in the  case of Greece lots of private  individuals and had assets that were  issued by the Greek government or issued  by Greece private individuals and so  those holdings lose value as you think  that they're less able to pay and so  there's a big ramification for people's  portfolios their perceptions of net  wealth that's one instance I'd say in  the case of Greece there was another set  of concerns and one of which is that the  euro was built upon the concept that no  government could default that every  government was going to be embarked upon  a path of sustainable finances okay so  the very fact that a country within the  euro area would get into trouble and  then would have to be bailed out which  is against you know that that's written  out of the the Charter for the eurozone  so that sort of forced a reassessment of  what is possible and if you think about  it it's not just Greece it's a Greece  signals what could happen to the other  periphery countries so Portugal Italy  Ireland Spain you know those other  countries also had problems with their  finances and it's not just Greece going  out but the signal  that Greece gives about whether those  countries could also face a similar  problem have to be bailed out and what  that means for the sustainability of the  the single European currency and you're  watching book TV on c-span - and we've  been talking with University of  Wisconsin professor Minzy Jim he's the  co-author of this book lost decades the  making of America's debt crisis and long  recovery Thank You professor thank you
 

